On 9 April 2011 18:11, Luke Bergen <lukeabergen@gmail.com> wrote:
> I'm confused about the difference between the schwa ("a" in "a dog") and =
the
> "u" in "rut". =C2=A0I hear those two as the same I think.

You're either mispronouncing one of the words or both of them, or
you're mishearing.


If "rut" was pronounced with a schwa, like [=C9=B9=C9=99t] (instead of [=C9=
=B9=C9=90t]),
it would sound like this:
http://www.mediafire.com/?v7vdzkn22ahmfn2

If "cut" was pronounced with a schwa, like [k=C9=99t] (instead of [k=C9=90t=
]),
it would sound like this:
http://www.mediafire.com/?whl4if98b9741pb

If "nut" was pronounced with a schwa, like [n=C9=99t] (instead of [n=C9=90t=
]),
it would sound like this:
http://www.mediafire.com/?isvcl8uc0x7e9i8


On the other hand, if "a dog" was pronounced with [=C9=90], like [=C9=90.d=
=C9=92g]
(instead of [=C9=99.d=C9=92g]), it would sound like this:
http://www.mediafire.com/?786nu3l7p8ito6r


Also, "o" in "do" is [=CA=8A] (actually [=CA=8A=CB=90]) - a sound better su=
ited for
lojban {u}. "o" in "for" is better for lojban {o}.

You can get much better acquainted with all this phonetic mumbo-jumbo here:
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:IPA

